# sapintegrationsuite_user_credential (Resource)

A user credentials artifact holds a user name and password that adapters use for basic or
username-token authentication against a receiver. Integration flows reference it by its name
(`id`), the credential alias. The password is a write-only attribute: Terraform sends it to SAP
but never stores it in plan or state, and SAP never returns it.

**Status:** partial. SAP documents creating, reading, updating and deleting user credentials.
`kind` and `company_id` come from the tenant's `$metadata`, and their accepted values beyond a
generic credential are not documented; the deployment status SAP shows in the UI is not
exposed.

## Prerequisites

- An OAuth client with `CredentialsEdit` (`MonitoringDataRead` for the data source).
- Terraform 1.11 or later for the write-only `password_wo`.
- The password from your secret store, passed through a variable. Never write it into a `.tf`
  file.

## Lifecycle

| Operation | What happens |
|---|---|
| Create | Creates and deploys the credential with `user` and `password_wo`. |
| Read | Reads user, description, kind and company ID. The password is never read, so a password changed in the UI is not detected. |
| Update | Any change, including a new `password_wo_version`, redeploys the credential with `PUT`. SAP requires the secret on every edit, so the configured `password_wo` is sent every time. |
| Replacement | Changing `id` (the alias) or `kind` creates a new credential. Flows that reference the old alias stop working until they are changed. |
| Delete | Deletes the credential. Flows that use it fail at runtime afterwards. |

**Rotating the password.** Put the new password into `password_wo` and change
`password_wo_version`, for example to the rotation date. Terraform cannot see a change of the
write-only value itself, so without a new version nothing is sent.

